<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.14.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.8.2">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" Version="6.0.4">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="Moq" Version="4.20.72" />
    <PackageReference Include="FluentAssertions" Version="6.12.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    TEST-ONLY project references, and they are the whole of this project's graph.

    DnnMigration.Application brings Domain with it, so the service, validator, mapper and domain
    suites placed here by AAP 0.4.1.1 compile against everything they need.

    DnnMigration.Infrastructure is referenced for the Security and Infrastructure suites, and the
    reference is a reconciled decision rather than a convenience. AAP 0.5.2.2 draws the unit
    project with a single edge to Application; AAP 0.5.1.5 places
    Security/{BcryptPasswordHasher,JwtTokenService,PermissionEvaluator}Tests.cs here and names
    Infrastructure/Security/*.cs as their subject. Those two statements cannot both be honoured,
    and the resolution is to keep the edge, because the alternative deletes coverage rather than
    relocating it: LoggingAuditSink, LegacyCredentialVerifier, JwtTokenService and
    DatabaseHealthCheck are internal sealed types whose behaviour is asserted here with
    substituted collaborators and no host at all.

    The layering guarantee is unaffected. It is a property of what the four PRODUCTION project
    files reference - Domain declares zero project references, Application cannot see
    Infrastructure, and either violation is a compile error - and a test project is the outermost
    consumer of that graph, not a layer within it.

    WHAT THIS EDGE DOES NOT LICENCE. A unit test must not reimplement a component and then assert
    that its own double behaves as specified; that is a tautology which stays green while the
    shipped type breaks. Suites that need the real thing END TO END resolve it from the composed
    container in DnnMigration.IntegrationTests/Security/ - the password hasher, the token service,
    the permission evaluator and the refresh-token store are all proven there, through
    AddInfrastructure, which asserts the registration as well as the behaviour. Coverage in the
    two projects is complementary and neither is a substitute for the other.

    Do not add DnnMigration.Api. The Api layer is the integration project's subject, it is where
    the hosted pipeline and the authorisation policies live, and a unit test that started a host
    would stop being one.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\DnnMigration.Application\DnnMigration.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\DnnMigration.Infrastructure\DnnMigration.Infrastructure.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
